"""Module 2, Lesson 8: Shell Automation & Practical Bash."""

LESSON = {'id': '2-8', 'title': 'Shell Automation & Practical Bash', 'summary': 'Turn repeated Linux commands into small, understandable Bash scripts using shebangs, variables, quoting, exit codes, conditionals, loops, arguments, environment variables, and safe automation habits.', 'duration_minutes': 75, 'xp': 80, 'audio_script': 'Welcome to Lesson 2.8: Shell Automation and Practical Bash.\n\nRepeated manual work eventually becomes inconsistent manual work. Automation can make a known procedure repeatable, observable, and reviewable. But automation also scales mistakes, so the goal is not to automate everything. The goal is to encode work you understand with explicit inputs, validation, safe behavior, and verification.\n\nBash is a common Unix shell and scripting language. A script is a text file containing commands. A common first line is #!/usr/bin/env bash. That shebang tells the operating system which interpreter to use when the file is executed directly.\n\nA script can also be run explicitly with bash script.sh. To run ./script.sh directly, it normally needs execute permission. You already know how to add owner execute permission with chmod u+x.\n\nVariables store values. In a simple assignment such as name=Ascend, there are no spaces around the equals sign. Reference the value as $name or ${name}.\n\nQuoting is one of the most important shell habits. Double quotes allow variable expansion while keeping the resulting value together. Single quotes preserve most text literally. Unquoted expansions can undergo word splitting and filename expansion. Quote variable expansions by default unless you deliberately need shell interpretation.\n\nUnix commands return an exit status. Zero conventionally means success. Nonzero represents failure or another exceptional result. $? contains the most recent exit status. Scripts should return meaningful statuses so humans, CI systems, and other automation can detect failure.\n\nConditionals let scripts make decisions. Test whether a file exists, a directory exists, a value is empty, or a command succeeded. Validate required inputs before making changes.\n\nLoops repeat defined work. A for loop can iterate over files or environments. A while loop can repeat while a condition remains true or read input. Because loops multiply an action, quoting and assumptions matter even more.\n\nPositional arguments make inputs explicit. $1 is the first argument, $2 the second, $# the argument count, and "$@" represents all arguments safely when quoted. A deployment script should reject missing required inputs rather than silently guessing production.\n\nEnvironment variables are another configuration mechanism. export makes a shell variable available to child processes. Services, containers, CI jobs, and interactive shells may all receive different environments.\n\nEnvironment variables are not magically secure. Secrets can leak through logs, debug output, shell history, CI output, or careless echo commands. Check whether a sensitive variable exists without printing its value.\n\nYou will often see Bash safety settings such as set -e, set -u, and set -o pipefail. They can expose failures earlier: unset variables, failed commands, or failures hidden inside pipelines. They are useful, but Bash has nuanced behavior. Strict mode does not replace explicit validation and testing.\n\nFunctions can make repeated script logic readable. Keep them purposeful. A ten-line script does not need to become a framework.\n\nAnother key idea is idempotence. Ask what happens if the automation runs twice. Ensuring a directory exists with mkdir -p is naturally repeatable. Blindly appending the same configuration line every run can create duplicates. This idea becomes central later in infrastructure as code and configuration management.\n\nUseful scripts log what they are checking and whether they succeeded, especially in CI. Useful logging does not print secrets.\n\nTreat shell automation as code. Put appropriate scripts in Git. Review changes. Test success and failure paths. Tools such as ShellCheck can detect many common mistakes. A short Bash file can still have production-sized consequences.\n\nFor this lab, build a read-only Ascend preflight script. It will accept a directory, verify required files, report disk evidence, and return nonzero when requirements are not met. Then intentionally give it bad input and prove the failure is observable.\n\nAutomation should reduce uncertainty, not merely reduce typing.\n\nIn the capstone, you will combine this mindset with the rest of Module 2 to restore a failed service.\n\nKeep climbing.', 'objectives': ['Create and execute a small Bash script with a shebang.', 'Use variables, quoting, arguments, conditionals, and loops safely.', 'Use meaningful exit statuses to expose failure.', 'Use environment variables without leaking secrets.', 'Apply strict-mode, idempotence, testing, Git review, and safe logging.'], 'content': [{'heading': 'Automation encodes a known procedure', 'body': 'Automate after you understand the work. Clear inputs, validation, safe defaults, and verification matter more than cleverness.'}, {'heading': 'Quote expansions by default', 'body': 'Unquoted variables can be split or expanded as filename patterns. Quoting protects the value you intended.'}, {'heading': 'Exit status makes failure observable', 'body': 'Zero conventionally means success; nonzero lets humans and systems react to failure.'}, {'heading': 'Arguments should remove ambiguity', 'body': 'Validate required inputs and reject dangerous defaults instead of guessing.'}, {'heading': 'Environment is configuration', 'body': 'Child processes can inherit exported variables, but services and CI may have different environments than your shell.'}, {'heading': 'Strict mode helps but is not magic', 'body': 'set -e, set -u, and pipefail can surface errors but do not replace deliberate error handling.'}, {'heading': 'Treat scripts like production code', 'body': 'Version, review, test, and lint automation; ask whether repeated execution is safe and whether logs expose secrets.'}], 'diagram': {'title': 'Automation encodes a known procedure', 'description': 'Turn repeated Linux commands into small, understandable Bash scripts using shebangs, variables, quoting, exit codes, conditionals, loops, arguments, environment variables, and safe automation habits.', 'nodes': [{'label': 'Automation encodes a known procedure', 'detail': 'Automate after you understand the work. Clear inputs, validation, safe defaults, and verification matter more than cleverness.'}, {'label': 'Quote expansions by default', 'detail': 'Unquoted variables can be split or expanded as filename patterns. Quoting protects the value you intended.'}, {'label': 'Exit status makes failure observable', 'detail': 'Zero conventionally means success; nonzero lets humans and systems react to failure.'}, {'label': 'Arguments should remove ambiguity', 'detail': 'Validate required inputs and reject dangerous defaults instead of guessing.'}, {'label': 'Environment is configuration', 'detail': 'Child processes can inherit exported variables, but services and CI may have different environments than your shell.'}, {'label': 'Strict mode helps but is not magic', 'detail': 'set -e, set -u, and pipefail can surface errors but do not replace deliberate error handling.'}], 'caption': 'Orient, gather evidence, make the smallest justified change, and verify.'}, 'engineer_perspective': {'title': 'Engineer’s Perspective', 'body': 'Version, review, test, and lint automation; ask whether repeated execution is safe and whether logs expose secrets.'}, 'try_it_yourself': {'title': 'Try It Yourself', 'intro': 'Practice in an authorized lab environment and avoid exposing credentials or changing real production services.', 'steps': ['Create ~/ascend-linux-lab/scripts/ascend-preflight.sh.', 'Add #!/usr/bin/env bash and set -euo pipefail.', 'Require exactly one directory argument and print usage on missing input.', 'Verify the argument points to a directory.', 'Loop over app.conf and version.txt and report whether each exists.', 'Report du -sh and df -h evidence for the supplied path.'], 'takeaway': 'Explain what each command proves before you use it.'}, 'lab': {'title': 'Lesson 2.8 Lab', 'instructions': ['Create ~/ascend-linux-lab/scripts/ascend-preflight.sh.', 'Add #!/usr/bin/env bash and set -euo pipefail.', 'Require exactly one directory argument and print usage on missing input.', 'Verify the argument points to a directory.', 'Loop over app.conf and version.txt and report whether each exists.', 'Report du -sh and df -h evidence for the supplied path.', 'Make the script executable and run it against a safe test directory.', 'Rename one required file and prove the script reports failure with a nonzero exit.', 'Restore the file and rerun the preflight.', 'Explain what Git review, test cases, environment assumptions, and secret-safe logging would be required before CI use.', 'Answer what happens when the script runs twice and identify its idempotent operations.']}, 'quiz': [{'question': 'What does a Bash shebang do?', 'choices': ['Selects the interpreter for direct execution', 'Sets ownership', 'Starts systemd', 'Creates SSH keys'], 'correct': 0}, {'question': 'What does exit status 0 conventionally mean?', 'choices': ['Success', 'Root access', 'Syntax error', 'SIGKILL'], 'correct': 0}, {'question': 'Why quote variable expansions?', 'choices': ['Reduce unintended word splitting and globbing', 'Make variables secret', 'Enable sudo', 'Create a PID'], 'correct': 0}, {'question': 'What is $1?', 'choices': ['The first positional argument', 'The current PID only', 'The root directory', 'The first environment variable'], 'correct': 0}, {'question': 'What does export do?', 'choices': ['Makes a variable available to child processes', 'Commits it to Git', 'Encrypts it', 'Makes it root-owned'], 'correct': 0}, {'question': 'Why reject a missing required environment argument instead of defaulting to production?', 'choices': ['It reduces dangerous ambiguity', 'Production is always offline', 'Bash cannot use defaults', 'Arguments cannot be strings'], 'correct': 0}, {'question': 'What does set -u help detect?', 'choices': ['Use of unset variables', 'High CPU', 'SSH key changes', 'Merge conflicts'], 'correct': 0}, {'question': 'What does pipefail help expose?', 'choices': ['Failures earlier in a pipeline', 'Only successful commands', 'Ownership changes', 'DNS records'], 'correct': 0}, {'question': 'What does idempotence ask?', 'choices': ['Whether repeated execution remains safe and predictable', 'Whether a script is ten lines', 'Whether variables are global', 'Whether root owns it'], 'correct': 0}, {'question': 'How should shell automation be treated?', 'choices': ['As code deserving testing, review, version control, and safe logging', 'As disposable text', 'As a replacement for understanding', 'As always requiring root'], 'correct': 0}], 'reflection': 'Automation reduces variation but increases blast radius. Which design, testing, review, and failure-handling practices would make you trust a Bash script in a deployment pipeline?'}
